theory.intro:
To graph $y = \dfrac{1}{f(x)}$ from the graph of $y = f(x)$, read off the zeros, sign, turning points and end behaviour of $f(x)$. Each feature has a matching feature on the reciprocal graph.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
  xmin=-2.5, xmax=4.5, ymin=-4, ymax=4,
  xtick=\empty, ytick={-1,1},
  samples=200, clip=false,
  axis line style={-{Stealth}}, width=9cm, height=6.5cm]
\addplot[dashed, thick, blue, <->, domain=-2.5:4.5] {0.5*x - 0.75};
\addplot[thick, red, <->, domain=-2.5:1.0] {2/(x - 1.5)};
\addplot[thick, red, <->, domain=2.0:4.5] {2/(x - 1.5)};
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-4:4] ({1.5},{x});
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.6pt, black] coordinates {(-0.5,-1) (3.5,1)};
\node[blue, right] at (axis cs:-2.4,-3.1) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[red, right] at (axis cs:2.55,2.9) {$y=\frac{1}{f(x)}$};
\node[right] at (axis cs:1.6,-3.1) {$x=a$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.question_text:
The graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown below with horizontal asymptote $y = -2$, vertical asymptote $x = 3$, $x$-intercept $(1, 0)$, and local maximum $(-1, 4)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
  xmin=-4, xmax=6, ymin=-5, ymax=6,
  xtick={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4},
  samples=100, clip=false,
  axis line style={-{Stealth}}, width=10cm, height=7cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=-4:1.92, restrict y to domain=-5:6, unbounded coords=jump] { (-2*(x - 1)*(x*x - 41))/((x - 3)*(x*x + 9)) };
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=4:6, restrict y to domain=-5:6, unbounded coords=jump] { (-2*(x - 1)*(x*x - 41))/((x - 3)*(x*x + 9)) };
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-5:6] ({3}, {x}) node[above right] {$x=3$};
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-4:6] (x, {-2}) node[below right] {$y=-2$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
For the reciprocal function $y = \dfrac{1}{f(x)}$, state its horizontal asymptote, vertical asymptote, $x$-intercept, and the coordinates and nature of its local extremum.
